CloudBurrow patch 60 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1564).

The bug: a jobs.list of the jobs after one (patch 0064) still cost what
every job of the project did. It read cloudburrow_job_order as GoogleSQL,
whose WHERE comparisons googlesqlite compiles to function calls, which
SQLite cannot answer from an index (patch 0019's QueryRawContext says
so), so the lookup of the cursor job and the list of the jobs after it
each visited every row; and the jobs named that were added before the
cursor were read with findJobsMetadata, as GoogleSQL too, which visited
every row of the jobs table. And before any of it, jobsListHandler read
every job (Project.Jobs), as when every job is asked for, and dropped
them. Measured on one instance with 5,000 jobs (the kind node at two
CPUs), a list of one job took 70 ms, 138 ms with 10,000 and 274 ms with
20,000.

And jobs.list read no minCreationTime or maxCreationTime, so a client
asking for the last minute's jobs, or a query of INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS
with a bound of creation_time, was answered every job (1.0 s at 10,000
jobs, measured the same way), and the front cut them.

The patch reads every job only when every job is asked for, and reads
cloudburrow_job_order as SQLite SQL: the cursor job by
its primary key, and the jobs after it by an index of the project and
sequence number, which it makes (CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS) the first
time; and it reads the named jobs by the jobs table's primary key, as it
reads those after the cursor (findJobRowsByID). jobs.list leaves out a
job whose creation time, in milliseconds, is outside minCreationTime and
maxCreationTime ("after or at", "before or at"); a job with no such time
(none, or seconds, kept by an engine before patch 0013) is listed, and
the front, which times the jobs it sends on, cuts the list again.

